many tanzanians watched this moment with  mixed feelings  their president samir sulu hassan  getting her coveted 19 johnson  johnson injection a clear departure from  the policies implemented  by her predecessor john magufuli who  died in march  from heart problems he was one of the  handful of world leaders  who downplayed the threat of the  pandemic enchant world health  organization calls for masks  and social distancing he also rejected  vaccines  insisting the disease could be dealt  with by prayer and local remedies  when president hassan was sworn in she  said things would change  i am a mother of four children a  grandmother a wife  and president and commander-in-chief of  tanzania  i would not take the vaccine and  sacrifice my life if it was harmful  when i have all these responsibilities  and people who depend on me  the country is in its third wave of the  disease officially it has so far  recorded only 29 deaths  and 858 cases this is the beginning of a  national vaccination campaign  health workers the elderly and those  with underlying conditions are on the  priority list tanzania has received more  than a million johnson and johnson doses  donated by the u.s through the world  health organization's coverts initiative  we are not late because even other  developed nations like europe usa  are still vaccinating so i think we are  okay  the vaccine is here it's voluntary there  are those who will decide to take it  and those who will not like everyone  else it's a personal decision  now in africa only eritrea and burundi  are not vaccinating  but fewer than two percent of the  african population  has been jobbed and it's highly unlikely  the continent  will meet a whole target of vaccinating  10 percent  by september this is partly because of a  shortage of vaccines  blamed on western nations holding  millions of doses  and anti-vaccine sentiments often fueled  by conspiracy theories  and mistrusts i i'm not ready yet on my  side  because i don't know there is all the  side effects of the vaccine  and there have been too many rumors  about it so i think  i'd rather look at others and  see what happens to them and that's when  i'll be ready or i'll be willing to have  the mexicans  tanzania is well behind many countries  in vaccination rates  but the government says it has set aside  470 million dollars  to secure more doses and hopes to  inoculate 60 percent of its population  as soon as possible catherine soy al  jazeera
 

